ield-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442554 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442554 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8ae518a-9958-5b32-a5fd-7a9d3da57763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442555 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442555 #edb/ref 1077 "field-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442555 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442555 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa9dd6f0-1904-5376-ac44-6fbcd00dad87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442556 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f204e80-4d56-5e54-8e44-fafc75ad1af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442556 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442557 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c4c6b4c-9f70-5e9c-b320-c7133b929a75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442557 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental matrix could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ce1dad2-2fca-5e8c-94e6-f02fef65fbf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442558 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a456bad8-fb03-5da0-aae9-4b577c9cb688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442559 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc97c5df-528b-5640-b7e6-250d27836f61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442560 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-t)={e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-t)={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-t{e}^{-t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(1+t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{-t}t+{e}^{-t}(1+t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336312" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ and substitution from line {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df1d5701-ba5a-54b3-9982-7187c6baef60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442561 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)],$ the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating each column}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its \ncolumns are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ Line $\\text{L1}$ identifies ${x}_{1}(t)$ as a column of $Φ(t),$ so ${x}_{1}(t)$ must satisfy the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L6}$ we have ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].$ Using $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$ from $\\text{L1},$ we substitute to obtain ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t).$ Thus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by substitution from $\\text{L1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442562 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffe871d4-6b88-566a-ad57-5a37491576bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442562 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
